CORRECTION to the earlier ding-waiter idea spooled 2026-08-20 from astro-polecam (file 20260820T195722Z-0C5VYB). That report said waiters 'die instantly' and blamed the session's background-task handling. PARTLY WRONG — split it in two:

(a) NOT A BUG, documented behaviour I misread: a waiter armed with --keep against a mailbox that STILL HOLDS delivered mail fires immediately and exits 0, because --keep deliberately does not truncate. The help says so: 're-arming with old mail present re-delivers it immediately'. So the session-start ritual ('arm with --keep', 'drain the spool') leaves a trap: strand-mailbox drain empties the SPOOL but does NOT clear MAILBOX.md, so every subsequent --keep re-arm self-fires and the Stop hook nags forever. Fix in the ritual, not the tool: after acting on mail, truncate MAILBOX.md before re-arming (or re-arm without --keep). Worth stating explicitly in the strand CLAUDE.md ritual text, since the two channels being 'ONE mailbox now' is exactly what makes this non-obvious.

(b) STILL UNEXPLAINED: three earlier arms on 2026-08-20 ~20:19-20:57 reported status 'killed' with EMPTY output files, while MAILBOX.md was 0 bytes and the spool was empty — so (a) cannot account for those; there was no mail to deliver. Pinning DING_OWNER=<claude pid> did not help; one was verified alive at 11s then killed. Two other sessions' identical waiters ran for hours concurrently. That half of the original report stands.
